## An [[amateur#Noun|amateur]] or someone who is [[underqualified]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Journal of Physical Education and Recreation|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:SHAPE America|American Alliance for Health, Physical Education, and Recreation]]|year=1962|volume=33|page=37|column=3|issn=0022-1473|oclc=1410487|passage=The point is that SCUBA diving is not for "'''rinky-dinks'''" ; it is for well trained people only, and then only in the company of other experienced divers.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Norman S. Pratt|title=Noble Conflict: A Vietnam War Novel: A Soldier Reflects on His Struggle with Issues of Morality and Integrity in a War Zone|location=Lincoln, Neb.|publisher={{w|iUniverse}}|year=2005|page=178|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OevHS7ON_YQC&pg=PA178|isbn=978-0-595-66825-0|passage=We've been ordered to some godforsaken place near Pleiku—the whole damn company, you and me and them other '''rinky-dinks''' back in Qui Nhon.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Jim Dent|authorlink=Jim Dent (author)|title=The Undefeated: The Oklahoma Sooners and the Greatest Winning Streak in College Football|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:St. Martin's Press|St. Martin’s Press]]|year=2001|isbn=978-0-312-26656-1|passage=Of course, most of the great players were at war and there was no need to pay the '''rinky-dinks''', who were merely occupying uniforms until the studs got back.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Eric J. DeMeulenaere|author2=Colette N. Cann|author3=with Chad R. Malone; James E. McDermott|quotee={{w|John Wooden}}|chapter=Introductiontitle=Reflections from the Field: How Coaching Made Us Better Teachers|location=Charlotte, N.C.|publisher={{w|Information Age Publishing}}|year=2013|page=15|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=lgQoDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA15|isbn=978-1-62396-269-2|passage=A player one time said, "You never let me play with Alcindor ([[w:Kareem Abdul-Jabbar|[Kareem] Abdul-Jabbar]]). I can do better if you let me play with him. Now you have me with some '''rinky-dinks'''." I told him one time, "That's what somebody said about you when you were in there. You were one of the '''rinky-dinks'''."}}
## Someone who [[operate]]s [[unethically]]; specifically, a [[small-time]] [[conman]] or [[crook#Noun|crook]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1958|title=News and Views|author=|page=30|isbn=|passage=In addition to such creeps, there is also found in various organizations an assortment of longhairs, corny antiques, arky back-numbers, moldy mossbacks, '''rinky-dinks''', tintypes, schmoes and schmaltzes of all flavors.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|title=Cobb: A Biography|author=Al Stump|page=|isbn=156512717X|passage=“I'm suing the Pacific Gas and Electric Company,” he explained, “for overcharging me on the service. Those '''rinky-dinks''' tacked an extra sixteen dollars on my bill.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|title=Starting Your Own Business|author=Jim Green|page=88|isbn=1857038592|passage=The '''rinky-dink''' is the 'no problem' merchant who causes no end of trouble for everyone else in the organisation. He will promise anything to land a sale and in the process lands himself in the drink.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} Something that is not up to [[acceptable]] [[standard#Noun|standards]]; something regarded as being of [[low#Adjective|low]] [[quality#Noun|quality]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1958|title=Proceedings [of The] Measurement of Management Conference|author=Society for Advancement of Management|page=|isbn=|passage=But when we start getting really into the "'''rinky-dinks'''" of this thing is when we get over here in the columns}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1970|title=To the Regiment|author=Ford E. Young|page=|isbn=|passage=Starting in late June of 1950, there were lots of "'''rinky-dinks''' to be unscrambled."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1975|title=Macroeconomic Issues and the Fiscal Year 1976 Budget: Seminars|author=United States Congress Senate Committee on the Budget|page=|isbn=|passage=The candor with which Ford constructed and presented his admittedly depressing Budget deprived the press of one of its favorite pastimes, namely, searching out the "'''rinky-dinks'''" in the document. In the past, such "'''rinky-dinks'''" existed partly in highly unrealistic assumptions concerning Congressional action requested by the incumbent President, or by the donning of rose-colored glasses in making economic assumptions.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|title=Mechanix Illustrated - Volume 76, Issues 620-625|author=|page=112|isbn=|passage=Soon afterward, the federal government, in effect, outlawed the '''rinky-dinks''' by adopting minimum-strength standards.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[miscellaneous]] [[object#Noun|object]] or [[thing]]; a [[thingy#Noun|thingy]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:thingy}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1915|title=The Motor World - Volume 45|author=|page=17|isbn=|passage=He wanted no detachable top of plebian characteristics; he wanted two regular bodies, one for winter and one for summer. And he wanted a special color and a monogram and a whole lot of '''rinky-dinks''' that cost him money.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1973|title=Loose Ends|author=Barbara Raskin|page=240|isbn=|passage=Coco was definitely a fifties person, not even in the same ballpark as the truly liberated young girls who let their children run barefoot and bareass through the park with their little pink '''rinky-dinks''' bobbing up and down.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|title=Levinas and the Cinema of Redemption: Time, Ethics, and the Feminine|author=Sam B. Girgus|page=|isbn=0231519494|passage=Marilyn Monroe who was blonde and beautiful and had a sweet little '''rinky-dink''' of a voice and all the cleanliness of all the clean American backyards.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|uncountable}} [[deceptive|Deceptive]] or [[underhanded]] [[rigmarole#Noun|rigmarole]]; [[deception]], [[trickery]]; {{lb|en|countable}} ''often preceded by'' '''[[the#Article|the]]''': an [[instance#Noun|instance]] of this; a deception, [[swindle#Noun|swindle]], a [[trick#Noun|trick]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:deception}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1901|title=The Chronicle - Volumes 67-68|author=|page=270|isbn=|passage=Two years of his life were spent at this elevating game, during which time he had only eighty-two fights with fond mothers who felt that he had been either neglectful of their particular children or had ruthlessly given them the '''rinky-dink'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1940|title=Verbatim Record of the Proceedings of the Temporary National Economic Committee|author=United States Temporary National Economic Committee|page=|isbn=|passage=You have this triennial audit or examination and in the cases of the fringe companies like we had yesterday, without any real notice to stockholders or any certificate of an auditing firm, if they are interested in manipulation or '''rinky-dink''' of any kind they have got three years practically to get away from it unless somebody picks it up out of the annual report.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1959|title=Housing Act of 1959|author=United States Congress House Banking and Currency Committee|page=|isbn=|passage=... , if they have to do that there is no point in going through this '''rinky-dink''' of trading bonds for mortgages, you just go out and sell the bond and take your loss and do what you want to do with the money, ...}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1984|title=How the Financial System Can Best be Shaped to Meet the Needs of the American People|author=United States Congress House Committee on Banking, Finance, and Urban Affairs|page=|isbn=|passage=Then it goes on to explain all of the '''rinky-dinks''' that have occurred in the mergers and gold parachutes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1973|title=The Bar Examiner - Volume 42|author=|page=79|isbn=|passage=It became necessary for the State Board of Law Examiners to overrule the local board on that point. We are proud of him in that he didn't go through the '''rinky-dink''' that he said he had an office in his home and would practice at night.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|title=F. D. R.: An Intimate History|author=Nathan Miller|page=80|isbn=0819180610|passage=What's the matter with Roosevelt and his Plan? All the other reformers have them on the pan. Fattened them up with printer's ink. Then handed them the '''rinky dink'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|title=Treadmill to Oblivion|author=Fred Allen|page=176|isbn=1434454126|passage=He was givin' her the old '''rinky dink''', eh?}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|music}} {{alternative form of|en|rinky-tink|t=a [[tinkling#Adjective|tinkling]], [[tinny#Adjective|tinny]] [[style#Noun|style]] of [[music]]; [[honky-tonk]]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1947|title=Frontiers of Jazz|author=Ralph de Toledano|page=119|isbn=1455604674|passage=The Oliver band had a new kind of beat, a real jazz beat instead of the ragtime '''rinky-dink'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|title=Ignorant Armies|author=Donald Murray Fraser & Bryan Carson|page=124|isbn=|passage=Devon on the run, then. No longer playing '''rinky-dink''' in fag clubs, pumping the organ in skidroad missions, humping ivories for small change and beer.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|title=Thimio's House|author=John Kefala Kerr|page=|isbn=1782790527|passage=In his symphony, the form of the notes is the form of the moment, and the form of the moment is strings (lustre), horn (sheen), oboe (edge), harp (light) and glockenspiel (a touch of the '''rinky-dink''').}}

